{:fh/id  "FH-CTRL-018"
 :fh/law "In a real browser the first-party controls hold the user-visible facts a structural assertion cannot reach: sustained and mid-string typing through a `field` produces a value equal to the typed string character for character with the caret where the user put it, a `buffered-field`'s Enter COMMITS while an Enter carrying a live IME composition commits nothing and leaves the draft alone, and a caller's reset restores the baseline on the SAME node — still focused, and through the controlled-input path rather than by remounting."

 :frame :rf.test/controls-kit
 :form-path [:editor]

 :baseline {:amount "10"}
 :amount-path [:amount]

 ;; SUSTAINED TYPING. Appended one character at a time, the way a user
 ;; produces it, so a dropped keystroke shows up as a short string rather
 ;; than as a timing observation.
 :typed "12345"
 :value-after-typing "1012345"

 ;; A MID-STRING INSERT. The caret is placed inside the text, one
 ;; character arrives, and the caret must follow it rather than jumping to
 ;; either end — the failure a naive controlled input produces on every
 ;; keystroke.
 :insert-at 1
 :insert-ch "9"
 :value-after-insert "190"
 :caret-after-insert 2

 ;; THE COMMIT. A plain Enter commits the draft the form is holding.
 :commit-key "Enter"
 :commits-after-plain-enter 1
 :committed-value "190"

 ;; THE COMPOSING ENTER. The same key, with a composition in flight — the
 ;; Enter that accepts an input-method candidate. `keyCode` 229 is the
 ;; sentinel every engine emits for it, and `isComposing` is the standard
 ;; flag; both are set, because a control that trusted only one of them is
 ;; the control this row exists to catch.
 :composing-key-code 229
 :commits-after-composing-enter 0

 ;; And the draft is UNTOUCHED by the composing Enter — a commit withheld
 ;; must not also be a value discarded.
 :value-after-composing-enter "190"

 ;; THE RESET. The caller rejects by reasserting the value it already had
 ;; and advancing the leaf's generation, which is the case value-equality
 ;; is blind to.
 :reasserted-value "10"
 :next-reset-key 1
 :value-after-reset "10"

 ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 ;; The roster record (rf2-drpa3.182.7)
 ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 ;;
 ;; `re-frame.freehand.roster` joins this with the row that addresses the
 ;; id and answers ONE value naming source, tiers, evidence and prose. The
 ;; fields the index already carries — modes, hosts, status, paragraph —
 ;; are deliberately absent: they are read from the row, not restated.
 :fh/record
 {:source [re-frame.freehand.form re-frame.freehand.controls]

  ;; NO structural tier, and that is the law rather than an omission. This
  ;; row opens "In a real browser" and the index binds it to `interpreted
  ;; browser`: a caret position, a live IME composition and a still-focused
  ;; node are facts a headless walk cannot reach. The kit's PURE
  ;; transitions are proven headlessly, but by FH-CTRL-012..017 — a
  ;; different set of laws with their own ids. Naming
  ;; `controls-kit-cljs-test` here would have made this row claim a
  ;; structural proof that suite never offers.
  :mounted
  [{:ns  re-frame.freehand.controls-kit-dom-cljs-test
    :law "Against real key events in Chromium the caret follows the user rather than jumping to either end, a plain Enter commits through the ordinary batched path while an Enter carrying a live IME composition commits nothing and discards nothing, and a caller's reset restores the baseline on the SAME still-focused node rather than by remounting it."}]

  ;; A control kit's evidence is what a run leaves for a reader: the value
  ;; the DOM node actually held, and the count of commits that reached
  ;; dispatch. Both are read off the substrate rather than off the test's
  ;; own bookkeeping.
  :evidence
  {:reads    [:dom/value :dom/selection-start]
   :counts   [:form/commits :form/attempts]
   :residue  :none}

  :prose :executable}}
